WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:06.740
In September 1522, a battered ship carrying just 18 men entered a Spanish port. Three years earlier, the Victoria

2
00:00:06.741 --> 00:00:14.398
had sailed from Spain with over two hundred sailors in search of precious spices. The Victoria was one of a

3
00:00:14.399 --> 00:00:26.881
fleet of five ships commanded by Ferdinand Magellan, an adventurer so hungry to explore the world that he had

4
00:00:26.882 --> 00:00:44.169
left his native Portugal to lead a Spanish expedition. His fleet had nearly been forgotten over its long voyage,

5
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:49.263
but its return marked the beginning of a new era, for the Victoria carried much more than spices&#8212;it carried

6
00:00:49.264 --> 00:00:53.319
the legacy of Ferdinand Magellan, whose amazing expedition had sailed all the way around the globe. Magellan was born in

7
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.866
1480 to Portuguese parents of noble ancestry. By age 12, he had become a page in the royal court

8
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:04.421
of Portugal. There, he studied swordsmanship, mapmaking, and navigation. Such skills were critical in the Age of Discovery, when

9
00:01:04.422 --> 00:01:15.361
men sailed the seas in search of riches and glory. When Christopher Columbus arrived in the New World and

10
00:01:15.362 --> 00:01:23.790
claimed it for Spain in 1492, Magellan was captivated by the idea of new and exotic lands. In 1499, Portuguese

11
00:01:23.791 --> 00:01:31.066
explorer Vasco da Gama discovered a sea route to India, and Magellan was further inspired. Magellan dreamed that he,

12
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:43.159
too, might become an explorer. In 1505, he joined the Portuguese navy. During the next eight years, he learned

13
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:53.758
seamanship and naval warfare, sailing east to India, China, and Malaysia. He returned to Portugal determined to lead an expedition

14
00:01:53.759 --> 00:02:01.139
of his own. After the discovery of the New World in 1492, Spain and Portugal, the greatest maritime powers

15
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.628
in Europe, both wanted to control the lands yet to be found. To resolve the conflict, Spain and Portugal

16
00:02:06.629 --> 00:02:15.415
signed a treaty dividing the entire non-European world in two. A line was drawn on a map over the

17
00:02:15.416 --> 00:02:24.249
Atlantic Ocean, from north to south. Spain was given rights to all land west of the line, while Portugal claimed

18
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.723
the land to the east, including a portion of what is now Brazil. Spain and Portugal, however, had both

19
00:02:27.724 --> 00:02:33.777
been sailing to the Spice Islands (now a part of Indonesia) to buy nutmeg, pepper, and other spices. Who

20
00:02:33.778 --> 00:02:39.219
should now control the islands was hotly contested. Portugal sailed east to the islands, but Magellan believed there was

21
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:47.649
a way to the islands through the New World. He brought his idea to Manuel I, the king of Portugal,

22
00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:54.115
but the king rejected it. Angered, Magellan left Portugal and proposed his idea to Charles V, the king of

23
00:02:54.116 --> 00:03:04.153
Spain, who agreed to pay for an expedition. But Charles did not fully trust the Portuguese Magellan, so he

24
00:03:04.154 --> 00:03:11.980
supplied Spanish men for the crew. Magellan commanded the Trinidad , but Spanish officers commanded the other ships&#8212;the Victoria, San

25
00:03:11.981 --> 00:03:22.641
Antonio, Concepci&oacute;n , and Santiago . Three of the Spanish captains believed that Magellan was really working for Portugal,

26
00:03:22.642 --> 00:03:28.729
and planned to kill him once they were at sea. On September 20, 1519, Magellan sailed his fleet west

27
00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:35.674
from Spain to the Canary Islands. There, he discovered the plot against him. He had the conspirators imprisoned aboard

28
00:03:35.675 --> 00:03:44.660
the Victoria and appointed three new captains. The fleet continued across the Atlantic Ocean, anchoring in Rio de Janeiro in

29
00:03:44.661 --> 00:03:55.655
December 1519. After taking on new supplies, the ships sailed along the coast of South America, but temperatures dropped

30
00:03:55.656 --> 00:04:03.140
and heavy storms battered the fleet. By March 1520, ice in the water and bitter cold threatened the expedition.

31
00:04:03.141 --> 00:04:08.876
Magellan ordered the ships to anchor near a rocky coast and wait for warmer weather. Again, his captains conspired to

32
00:04:08.877 --> 00:04:16.805
abandon the expedition and return to Spain, but Magellan discovered the plot. This time, he had one of the

33
00:04:16.806 --> 00:04:24.752
leaders of the mutiny executed and another marooned. Toward the end of winter, Magellan grew impatient and sent the

34
00:04:24.753 --> 00:04:32.294
Santiago along the coast in search of a strait through the continent. The ship was wrecked during a storm,

35
00:04:32.295 --> 00:04:38.079
and after rescuing the stranded crew, the remaining four vessels continued south. In October 1520, after nearly a year at

36
00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.290
sea, the fleet spotted a waterway through the landmass. Magellan named it the Strait of All Saints, but it

37
00:04:43.291 --> 00:04:53.999
would come to be known as the Strait of Magellan. Shallow waters and jagged rock formations threatened the safe

38
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.714
passage of the fleet. As the ships entered the strait, the San Antonio suddenly turned around, fleeing east with

39
00:05:00.715 --> 00:05:07.212
most of the fleet’s supplies. Magellan rallied the rest of his ships onward. After 38 days of painstaking navigation, the

40
00:05:07.213 --> 00:05:13.863
fleet emerged from the strait into open waters. Magellan named the sea rippling before him for its calmness, calling

41
00:05:13.864 --> 00:05:23.309
it the Pacific Ocean. He believed that he was just days from the Spice Islands, but he had no

42
00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:28.031
idea how vast the ocean was. The ships were low on supplies, and as they sailed west, conditions worsened. There

43
00:05:28.032 --> 00:05:33.924
was no fresh food, and the drinking water turned foul. Their biscuits were infested with maggots, but the men

44
00:05:33.925 --> 00:05:42.003
ate them anyway. When the biscuits were gone, the starving sailors ate rats, sawdust, and leather. Scurvy, a disease

45
00:05:42.004 --> 00:05:50.774
caused by lack of vitamin C, claimed the lives of some of the crew, while others died from starvation.

46
00:05:50.775 --> 00:05:57.782
Magellan’s ships sailed for three months, crossing the largest body of water anyone had ever seen, until they reached the

47
00:05:57.783 --> 00:06:04.823
island of Guam. The men took on supplies there and sailed again, arriving in the Philippines in March 1521.

48
00:06:04.824 --> 00:06:10.555
The Trinidad, Victoria, and Concepci&oacute;n were badly in need of repair, so Magellan lingered in the Philippines. While his

49
00:06:10.556 --> 00:06:16.093
crew mended sails and scrubbed decks, Magellan befriended the native people. A Christian, Magellan felt he had a duty

50
00:06:16.094 --> 00:06:21.196
to convert the natives to Christianity. When one island tribe refused, he decided to force them to adopt his faith.

51
00:06:21.197 --> 00:06:29.812
On April 27, 1521, he sailed to the island only to find that shallow waters prevented anchoring close to

52
00:06:29.813 --> 00:06:35.464
shore. So Magellan and sixty of his men walked through the thigh-high water toward the beach. Thousands of natives

53
00:06:35.465 --> 00:06:43.187
rushed at them, hurling spears. Magellan pressed on because he was certain his armor would protect him. But a poisoned-tip

54
00:06:43.188 --> 00:06:50.013
spear pierced Magellan’s leg, and the natives rushed to kill the fleet’s leader. There were only 115 crewmen left

55
00:06:50.014 --> 00:06:56.914
alive after the battle, not enough to crew three ships. They burned the Concepci&oacute;n, and the last two ships

56
00:06:56.915 --> 00:07:06.186
sailed for the Spice Islands on May 1, 1521&#8212;without Magellan. After loading the Trinidad and Victoria with valuable spices,

57
00:07:06.187 --> 00:07:18.053
the Victoria ’s captain decided to send one ship east and the other west, hoping to minimize the risk that

58
00:07:18.054 --> 00:07:26.768
both would perish. The Trinidad , sailing east, was captured by Portuguese sailors; the Victoria sailed west, around Africa’s

59
00:07:26.769 --> 00:07:32.418
southern coast. During the three-month voyage, storms and scurvy attacked the crew yet again. Finally, on September 6, 1522,

60
00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:44.811
this lone surviving vessel arrived in Spain, almost exactly three years after the fleet had sailed west for the

61
00:07:44.812 --> 00:07:52.415
Spice Islands. The expedition had covered 42,000 miles; Magellan had not found a faster way to the Spice Islands. He

62
00:07:52.416 --> 00:08:00.603
earned his place in history for something much greater&#8212;his expedition had proved that ships could sail around the globe.

